 "interpretation": {
  "stable_facts_L1": [
   "16 MiB pieces, legacy start (gsp02-v4 C0 deadlines), pb0-v2 mixed serving swarm: piece 0 completes at 12.89 s median [12.70-13.17] with two stalled peers versus 7.17 s [7.16-7.26] without (n=5 each); +5.7 s, no overlap. Silent and keep-alive ('chatty') stalled peers behave the same (12.90 s [12.70-13.16]).",
   "Every stalled peer (10/10, silent and chatty) was snubbed 9.98-9.99 s after its first block request: the native 1 s tick of piece_timeout (product value 10), not request_timeout (30; the per-peer request_timeout read 30) or peer_timeout (60). Keep-alives do not prevent it.",
   "In 5/5 stalled runs at 16 MiB, 1-2 blocks of piece 0 were requested only from a stalled peer until just before the snub; they left the stalled peer at the snub and piece 0 completed about 1.5 s later. Before the snub libtorrent 2.1.1 did not re-request them elsewhere (no time-critical duplicate request).",
   "4 MiB pieces: no piece-0 penalty (3.22 s vs 3.20 s median); pieces 0-2 together +0.6 s (4.61 vs 3.97 s). The window was done before the 10 s snub, the stalled peers held mostly non-first blocks; the stall cost grows with piece size (more of a piece's time is spent waiting).",
   "No serving peer was snubbed in any L1 run (0 false snubs at piece_timeout 10)."
  ],
  "hints_not_results": [
   "Contaminated L2 (two containers overlapped, 3 reps, 16 MiB): with piece_timeout 2/3/5 the stalled peers were snubbed 1.99/2.98/4.99 s after their first request, i.e. the snub time follows piece_timeout exactly. Piece 0: 5.69 s (pt2), 9.42 s (pt3), 9.43 s (pt5) versus ~12.9 s at 10. The 3 and 5 s values ended at the same ~9.4 s: after the snub the freed blocks were not re-requested at once.",
   "Probable reason (not yet tested): libtorrent's time-critical picker stops when the best peer already has more than 2 s of queued requests; with request_queue_time 3 every serving peer's queue is about 3 s deep, so freed deadline blocks wait for the regular picker / end-game. Candidate combination to probe: short piece_timeout plus request_queue_time 1 during streams.",
   "4 MiB with piece_timeout 2/3 in contaminated L2: no change (3.16-3.19 s piece 0), as expected when the stall did not touch piece 0.",
   "smoke3 (n=1, 16 MiB): piece_timeout 2 with chatty stalled peers: snub at 3.5 s, piece 0 at 5.66 s versus 12.88 s with product settings."
  ],
  "relation_to_pb0_v2": "pb0-v2 mixed (8 peers, 2 stalled) showed 13-18 s to the first byte. The raw-libtorrent probe reproduces a +5.7 s piece-0 penalty at 16 MiB purely from the stalled peers; the rest of the pb0 gap to the fast profile is lower swarm bandwidth (10.5 vs 32 MB/s) and connection set-up, not the stall.",
  "candidate_basis": "Only piece_timeout has a demonstrated (contaminated, n=3) effect so far; a stream-scoped shorter piece_timeout is the evidence-backed candidate shape. Its value (2 vs 3 s), the harm on serving peers without stalls (ctrl_piece_t2/t3), and the request_queue_time interaction are NOT yet measured cleanly; the candidate was therefore not committed.",
  "gsp10_gate_should_measure": [
   "H1 mixed profile (2 of 8 peers stalled), 4 and 16 MiB (1 MiB as a control) at 30 Mbit/s: time to first byte / time to start, product vs stream-scoped piece_timeout candidate.",
   "fast and thin profiles as regression controls: no J-exceeding loss of start time or throughput (false snubs of serving peers).",
   "Record per run: stalled-peer snub time after first request and serving-peer snub count (libtorrent peer_snubbed_alert), so a PASS/FAIL can be attributed to the mechanism.",
   "On top of whichever start policy GSP02 selects (the stall penalty adds to the start-order effect)."
  ],
  "limits": [
   "Synthetic loopback swarm, one client container with 2 CPUs; absolute times are harness-bound, only within-batch differences are meaningful.",
   "Stalled peers here never send data; slow-but-alive real peers with multi-second gaps would be snubbed too by a short piece_timeout (snub = request queue 1 and no time-critical requests until data arrives), which only the harm controls and the gate can quantify.",
   "piece_timeout is a session setting: a candidate must scope it to active streams to leave background downloads unchanged."
  ]
 }
